    translation: "At the university of applied sciences a petition circulated against the binding study advice in the first year, after a cohort had missed practical modules because of the long-term illness of two lecturers. The examination board maintained that the standard was not arbitrary, though it acknowledged that catch-up teaching had been organised too late. Students did not demand automatic passes, but a recalculation of the credit total without the cancelled practicals. In media reports the conflict quickly became a debate about a ‘snowflake generation’. Anyone who read the documents saw something narrower: a procedure that assumes a fully delivered programme, applied to a year that had not had that programme. The board feared a precedent; the students feared that leniency would only be distributed informally, and thus unequally. In the end there was an addendum: anyone who had demonstrably missed modules could take two resits outside the BSA window, without grades being raised. Nobody called it a victory. The student assessor wrote in the report that the tone of the petition had been sharper than the negotiation, and that precisely that sharpness had forced the council to record the exception in writing. The institution is HU, Built Environment institute, 2025-2026 cohort of 112 first-years. Two lecturers of the BIM and surveying practicals were off from week 8 to week 18. The examination board kept the BSA standard at 45 of 60 credits, while 38 students had demonstrably missed 8 practical credits. The petition gathered 640 signatures, 90 of them from outside the programme, which gave the media the snowflake frame. Addendum EXA-2026-07 states that resits in June and August fall outside the window, without grade inflation, and that the leniency applies only with a statement from the team leader. The student assessor dated the report 11 March, room 1.64, and put the year of the OER appendix into the minutes out loud.",
